/*
 * translations.js — string tables for the Prana Calendar UI.
 *
 * PC_TRANSLATIONS.<lang> is a flat { key: string } map. English (en) is
 * the source of truth and the fallback used whenever another language is
 * missing a key (see pcT() in app.js) — no key should ever be removed from
 * `en` without also removing it from the others, and no key should exist in
 * fr/nl/es that isn't in en.
 *
 * Strings with {placeholders} (e.g. "Page {current} of {total}") are
 * filled in by pcT(key, vars) at render time — keep the same placeholder
 * names when editing a translation.
 *
 * fr/nl/es were produced as a first machine-assisted pass (Google-Translate
 * quality) and should be reviewed by a native speaker before this goes in
 * front of real users. Sanskrit/technical terms (Chakra, Tithi, Nakshatra,
 * Paksha, Svar Yoga, Nada Yoga, Chilla, Ida, Pingala, Prana Calendar,
 * InnerTuning, Nitya, Muladhara/Svadhisthana/etc., mantra names, nakshatra
 * and nitya proper names) are intentionally left untranslated, as they are
 * used the same way internationally — see individual key comments below for
 * the exact groups this covers.
 *
 * WHAT'S IN HERE, beyond ordinary short UI strings:
 *   - learnP1..learnP87 — every paragraph of the Learn tab essays (data-i18n
 *     / data-i18n-html in index.html's #tab-learn). data-i18n-html entries
 *     (learnP27, learnP87) contain a literal <a href> link — see
 *     applyTranslations() in app.js, which sets these via innerHTML rather
 *     than textContent.
 *   - nakshatra_<0-26>_epithet / _themes / _description — the 27 nakshatras
 *     (js/nakshatra-data.js). The nakshatra's own `name`, `deity`, and
 *     `planet` fields are NOT translated here (proper Sanskrit/deity names
 *     with short English glosses) — see pcLocalizedNakshatra() in app.js.
 *   - nakshatraDiffNote — the Nakshatra-vs-Nitya explainer note.
 *   - nitya_<0-15>_epithet / _description — the 16 Nityas
 *     (js/nitya-data.js); `name`/`vowel`/`devanagari` stay untranslated.
 *   - nityaFootnote_classical / _nityotsava, nityaSchemeLabel_classical /
 *     _nityotsava — the Nitya accordion's footnote and scheme-toggle labels.
 *   - quiz_<pageKey>_q<index>[_opt<n>] — every Chakra Quiz question and
 *     multiple-choice option label (js/chakra-quiz-data.js), plus
 *     quiz_soma_purification / quiz_soma_purificationNote.
 *   - quizMantra_<chakraKey>_whySentence / _sustainMessage /
 *     _additionalMantras_<n> — the per-chakra results-page copy
 *     (PC_CHAKRA_MANTRAS). Mantra proper names inside these strings are
 *     preserved untranslated; only descriptive/parenthetical text is
 *     translated — see pcLocalizedMantraInfo() in app.js.
 *   - tier2Tie_hritThroat / _gayatriHigher, quizConsultationCta,
 *     quizDisclaimer — shared quiz results-page copy.
 *   - nostrilLetterLeft / nostrilLetterRight — the single-letter nostril
 *     abbreviation used in the Month View calendar grid, translated per the
 *     app's exact spec: EN L/R, FR G/D (Gauche/Droite), NL L/R
 *     (Links/Rechts), ES I/D (Izquierda/Derecha).
 *   - resultNostrilLeft / resultNostrilRight — the full "LEFT/RIGHT
 *     nostril" label shown on the Today result card.
 *   - shareBtn, shareFailedNote, share*Message — the Share feature's button
 *     label and per-surface message templates (see pcShareText() in
 *     app.js), each with a trailing {link} placeholder.
 *
 * NOT translated, by explicit scoping decision (documented in the delivery
 * notes) — left English-only: the deity/planet fields in nakshatra-data.js,
 * the full reference text in mantra-catalog.js (mostly proper mantra
 * names/URLs already), and a small number of low-visibility technical
 * strings (e.g. the rare polar-day/night note appended to result details).
 */
//
// Planet names (added: planet name translation + Nitya vowel phonetic
// guides round): the 7 classical Western planet names (Sun, Moon,
// Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn) ARE translated per-language,
// since they are common nouns in each language, not sacred proper names.
// Deity names (Brahma, Agni, Vishnu, Surya, Soma, Mangala, Budha, Guru,
// Brihaspati, Shukra, Shani, and the lunar nodes Rahu/Ketu) remain
// untranslated across all languages, consistent with this file's
// existing Sanskrit/proper-name policy above.
//
// Nitya vowel phonetic guides (nitya_0_vowel .. nitya_15_vowel): these
// re-spell the same Sanskrit vowel sound using each language's own
// orthographic conventions, so the pronunciation guide next to each
// Nitya's Devanagari character actually reads correctly aloud in that
// language, rather than carrying over English-only spelling habits
// (e.g. English's 'EE' for a long i is written 'i' in French/Spanish,
// since a plain 'i' already makes that sound there). One deliberate
// exception: Dutch renders the long i as 'ie' rather than a bare 'i',
// because Dutch orthography productively distinguishes short 'i' from
// long 'ie' (e.g. "dit" vs. "dief") -- a bare 'i' would read as the
// SHORT vowel to a Dutch speaker and mislead the pronunciation, working
// against the whole point of a phonetic guide. These are a first-pass,
// best-effort transliteration and -- like the rest of this file's
// translated content -- would benefit from native-speaker review.
//

